Deformable Effects — Frameweave, Apache-2.0.

Rust distance and isometric bending mathematics are adapted from the MIT
PositionBasedDynamics2.2.0 release, commit
aa62c44f0d43956452e1f960a40333ec2d6d3ea5.
Copyright (c) 2015-present, PositionBasedDynamics contributors.
Full MIT text and checksum-pinned reference files are in vendor/pbd.
No Eigen, GUI, full PBD application or third-party native binary is included.
No GPL Blender source or assets are used.

For the existing XPBD constraints, numerical adaptations are explicit: direct compliance; f64 hypot and positive
representable denominator checks rather than fixed scene/mass epsilons;
degenerate/inconsistent errors rather than a silent skip; quadratic isometric
Q stored as coef*K*K^T and evaluated relative to a shared vertex.
Core integration/checkpoints and C1 bytes-v1 session/Worker/RPC are reused.
Rust1.99.1 standard-library notices and source identity are carried in licenses.
The optional local C++ validation executable is not distributed.

Static sphere/plane sweep geometry, conservative halfspaces and projected
Coulomb velocity response in contacts.rs are authored Apache-2.0 code.
Mathematical reference: Mueller et al., Position Based Dynamics (2006),
section 3.4, https://matthias-research.github.io/pages/publications/posBasedDyn.pdf
No paper text/code, Eigen collision engine or additional native library is copied.

Tetrahedral signed-volume XPBD is adapted from the same pinned MIT source.
Its derivative explicitly includes 1/6; the upstream code omits that factor.
Unmodified upstream text/counterexample and independent analytic/finite-difference
verification are retained. This is not a claim of identical volume projections.
Soft-body mesh/density/topology preparation is authored Apache-2.0.

Signed dihedral XPBD uses the same fixed MIT PBD2.2.0/aa62c44f source's
solve_DihedralConstraint angle-gradient formulas (attributed there to Bridson).
Two additional complete original source files are archived unchanged. Rust adapts
them to signed atan2, local coordinate scaling, explicit XPBD compliance/lambda,
wrapped targets and movable common-edge vertices. The native unsigned PBD method
and its epsilon/fixed-opposite/small-angle counterexamples are retained; this is
not a claim that upstream directly implements the new signed XPBD semantics.
No new crate, Eigen/full physics engine or runtime dependency is introduced.

Cosserat PBD rods reuse the same pinned MIT release's PositionBasedElasticRods
stretch/shear and bend/twist kernels and first-order TimeIntegration formulas.
Rust scalar adaptation retains original dimensionless stiffness and 1e-6
regularizers, with checked finite/unit-quaternion packets, Result propagation
and hemisphere-safe candidate normalization. Quaternion pin/mesh interpolation,
explicit data snapshots and existing C1/Core integration are module adapters.
Original MIT sources/call sites and sha256 records are carried in vendor/pbd.
No additional third-party library or full anisotropic inertia solver is bundled.

0.6.0 thin-face normal/tangent quadratic drag and prescribed pressure are authored Frameweave Apache-2.0 SI force math in Rust, not copied Bullet/NvCloth/Jolt implementations. Existing fixed MIT PBD constraints/C1/Worker/Core Runner are reused; no new dependency, full third-party engine or separately licensed file included.
